<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of the equivalence between diffusion and flow models</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#biba.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022a</a>)</cite> as a special case of diffusion modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Gao, 2023</a>; <a href="#biba.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In the following, we will first outline the formulation of diffusion models and then substitute the specific parameterization of Flow Matching to demonstrate their compatibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a random variable <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from an unknown data distribution <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, a Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#biba.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#biba.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> defines a forward process that gradually transforms the data into a simple prior distribution, typically a Gaussian distribution. The conditional distribution of the noised variable <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> given the initial data <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a Gaussian transition kernel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, a sample <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at any time <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed through a reparameterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#biba.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon},~\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0}+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon},~\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are scalar functions of time, known as the noise schedule, that control the signal-to-noise ratio. Typically, <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases over time while <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, satisfying a condition such as <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}^{2}+\sigma_{t}^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}^{2}+\sigma_{t}^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> in Variance Preserving (VP) SDEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#biba.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020b</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#biba.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al. (2021)</a></cite> proves that the following stochastic differential equation (SDE) has the same transition distribution in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E15" title="In Appendix A Proof of the equivalence between diffusion and flow models ‣ WorldForge: Unlocking Emergent 3D/4D Generation in Video Diffusion Model viaTraining-Free Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) for any <math id="A1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}=f(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\mathbf{w}_{t},~\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}=f(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\mathbf{w}_{t},~\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard Wiener process. The drift coefficient <math id="A1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the diffusion coefficient <math id="A1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> can be derived using schedule parameters <math id="A1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}\log\alpha_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t},\quad g^{2}(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}\sigma_{t}^{2}}{\mathrm{d}t}-2\frac{\mathrm{d}\log\alpha_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}\sigma_{t}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}\log\alpha_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t},\quad g^{2}(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}\sigma_{t}^{2}}{\mathrm{d}t}-2\frac{\mathrm{d}\log\alpha_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}\sigma_{t}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">The generative process of diffusion models involves reversing this forward process. This can be achieved via a corresponding reverse-time SDE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020b</a>)</cite>. For more efficient generation, one can utilize the associated probability flow ordinary differential equation (PF-ODE), which shares the same marginal distributions as at each time <math id="A1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as that of the SDE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020b</a>)</cite>. This PF-ODE is given by:</p>
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=f(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=f(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">By relating the score function <math id="A1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to the noise term via <math id="A1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\approx-\frac{\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}{\sigma_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\approx-\frac{\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}{\sigma_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a neural network trained to predict the noise, the ODE becomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#biba.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A1.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=f(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}+\frac{g^{2}(t)}{2\sigma_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=f(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}+\frac{g^{2}(t)}{2\sigma_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.7" class="ltx_p">Now, let us consider the forward process in Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#biba.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022a</a>)</cite>. The path from a data point <math id="A1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to a noise sample <math id="A1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by a simple linear interpolation:</p>
<table id="A1.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}=(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}+t\cdot\bm{\epsilon},~\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}=(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}+t\cdot\bm{\epsilon},~\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.8" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. By comparing Eq. (<a href="#A1.E21" title="In Appendix A Proof of the equivalence between diffusion and flow models ‣ WorldForge: Unlocking Emergent 3D/4D Generation in Video Diffusion Model viaTraining-Free Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) with the general form of the diffusion forward process in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E16" title="In Appendix A Proof of the equivalence between diffusion and flow models ‣ WorldForge: Unlocking Emergent 3D/4D Generation in Video Diffusion Model viaTraining-Free Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), we can establish a direct correspondence by setting the diffusion schedule parameters as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=1-t\quad\text{and}\quad\sigma_{t}=t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=1-t\quad\text{and}\quad\sigma_{t}=t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.9" class="ltx_p">Substituting this specific parameterization into the definitions for <math id="A1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E18" title="In Appendix A Proof of the equivalence between diffusion and flow models ‣ WorldForge: Unlocking Emergent 3D/4D Generation in Video Diffusion Model viaTraining-Free Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), we derive the corresponding coefficients for this Flow Matching SDE:</p>
<table id="A1.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}\log(1-t)}{\mathrm{d}t}=\frac{-1}{1-t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>FM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}\log(1-t)}{\mathrm{d}t}=\frac{-1}{1-t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}(t^{2})}{\mathrm{d}t}-2\frac{-1}{1-t}t^{2}=\frac{2t}{1-t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>FM</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)=\frac{\mathrm{d}(t^{2})}{\mathrm{d}t}-2\frac{-1}{1-t}t^{2}=\frac{2t}{1-t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.10" class="ltx_p">Next, we insert these specific coefficients <math id="A1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>FM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>FM</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> into the PF-ODE formulation from Eq. (<a href="#A1.E20" title="In Appendix A Proof of the equivalence between diffusion and flow models ‣ WorldForge: Unlocking Emergent 3D/4D Generation in Video Diffusion Model viaTraining-Free Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>). To analyze the underlying dynamics, we consider the ideal case where the score is perfectly known, which is equivalent to replacing the model prediction <math id="A1.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> with the ground-truth noise <math id="A1.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}+\frac{g_{\mathrm{FM}}^{2}(t)}{2\sigma_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>FM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>FM</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{\mathrm{FM}}(t)\mathbf{x}_{t}+\frac{g_{\mathrm{FM}}^{2}(t)}{2\sigma_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{-1}{1-t}\mathbf{x}_{t}+\frac{2t}{2t\cdot(1-t)}\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{-1}{1-t}\mathbf{x}_{t}+\frac{2t}{2t\cdot(1-t)}\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\bm{\epsilon}-\mathbf{x}_{t}}{1-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\bm{\epsilon}-\mathbf{x}_{t}}{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\bm{\epsilon}-[(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}+t\cdot\bm{\epsilon}]}{1-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\bm{\epsilon}-[(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}+t\cdot\bm{\epsilon}]}{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{(1-t)\bm{\epsilon}-(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}}{1-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{(1-t)\bm{\epsilon}-(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}}{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{\epsilon}-\mathbf{x}_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{\epsilon}-\mathbf{x}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.11" class="ltx_p">This resultant vector field, <math id="A1.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=\bm{\epsilon}-\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=\bm{\epsilon}-\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, is precisely the time derivative of the Flow Matching path defined in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E21" title="In Appendix A Proof of the equivalence between diffusion and flow models ‣ WorldForge: Unlocking Emergent 3D/4D Generation in Video Diffusion Model viaTraining-Free Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). This equivalence demonstrates that the process prescribed by Flow Matching is a specific instance of the diffusion models, corresponding to the linear noise schedule <math id="A1.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=1-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=1-t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=t</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, Flow Matching can be formally viewed as a subset of the broader diffusion modeling framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Gao, 2023</a>; <a href="#biba.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Evaluation Metrics</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ seven complementary metrics to comprehensively evaluate video generation quality: FID and <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLIP}_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CLIP</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLIP}_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math> similarity for static scenes, FVD and <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CLIP</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math> for dynamic scenes, and ATE, RPE-T, and RPE-R for camera trajectory consistency. These metrics provide objective quantitative assessment across multiple dimensions including image realism, semantic consistency, temporal coherence, and camera motion fidelity.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Static Scene Evaluation</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fréchet Inception Distance (FID).</span>
FID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heusel et al., 2017</a>)</cite> measures image generation quality by comparing the distribution of real and generated images in the Inception-V3 feature space. We use an ImageNet-pretrained Inception-V3 model and extract 2048-dimensional features from the pool3 layer. The FID score is computed as:</p>
<table id="A2.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FID}=\|\mu_{r}-\mu_{g}\|^{2}+\text{Tr}(\Sigma_{r}+\Sigma_{g}-2(\Sigma_{r}\Sigma_{g})^{1/2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>FID</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FID}=\|\mu_{r}-\mu_{g}\|^{2}+\text{Tr}(\Sigma_{r}+\Sigma_{g}-2(\Sigma_{r}\Sigma_{g})^{1/2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are the mean vectors of real and generated image features, and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding covariance matrices.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP Similarity.</span>
CLIP similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite> evaluates the semantic similarity between generated and real images using vision-language pre-trained representations. We employ the CLIP ViT-B/32 model trained on 400 million image-text pairs. The similarity score is calculated as:</p>
<table id="A2.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CLIP}_{\text{sim}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\cos(f_{r,i},f_{g,i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>CLIP</mtext><mtext>sim</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CLIP}_{\text{sim}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\cos(f_{r,i},f_{g,i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{g,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{g,i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the L2-normalized 512-dimensional CLIP features of the <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th real and generated image pair.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Dynamic Scene Evaluation</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fréchet Video Distance (FVD).</span>
FVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#biba.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Unterthiner et al., 2018</a>)</cite> extends FID to the video domain by measuring distribution differences in spatio-temporal feature space. For computational efficiency, we implement a simplified version that treats video frame sequences as image collections and applies Inception-V3 frame-wise feature extraction. The FVD score follows the same Fréchet distance formula as FID but operates on video frame features.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Video CLIP Similarity (<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CLIP</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">V</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CLIP</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math> extends CLIP similarity to the temporal domain by computing frame-level semantic consistency between generated and real videos. The score is calculated as:</p>
<table id="A2.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\left[\frac{1}{T_{j}}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{j}}\cos(f_{r,j,t},f_{g,j,t})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>CLIP</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\left[\frac{1}{T_{j}}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{j}}\cos(f_{r,j,t},f_{g,j,t})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of video pairs, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the frame count of the <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th video pair, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r,j,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r,j,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{g,j,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{g,j,t}</annotation></semantics></math> are the CLIP features of the <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th frame in the <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th video pair.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Camera Trajectory Evaluation</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absolute Trajectory Error (ATE).</span>
ATE measures the global consistency between estimated and reference camera trajectories by computing Euclidean distances between corresponding camera positions. The ATE for each timestamp is computed as:</p>
<table id="A2.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ATE}_{i}=\|\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}-\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}\|_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>ATE</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ATE}_{i}=\|\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}-\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the 3D position vectors of the reference and estimated trajectories at timestamp <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The root mean square error over all <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> trajectory points is:</p>
<table id="A2.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ATE}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\text{ATE}_{i}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>ATE</mtext><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mtext>ATE</mtext><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ATE}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\text{ATE}_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relative Pose Error - Translation (RPE-T).</span>
RPE-T evaluates the local accuracy of camera translation between consecutive frames, reflecting short-term trajectory consistency. For consecutive frames, the relative translation error is:</p>
<table id="A2.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RPE-T}_{i}=\|\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}-\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}\|_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>RPE-T</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RPE-T}_{i}=\|\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}-\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}=\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i+1}-\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}=\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i+1}-\mathbf{t}_{\text{ref},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}=\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i+1}-\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}=\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i+1}-\mathbf{t}_{\text{est},i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The RMSE over all <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive frame pairs is:</p>
<table id="A2.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RPE-T}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\text{RPE-T}_{i}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RPE-T</mtext><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mtext>RPE-T</mtext><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RPE-T}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\text{RPE-T}_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relative Pose Error - Rotation (RPE-R).</span>
RPE-R assesses the accuracy of camera orientation changes between consecutive frames. For rotation matrices <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the angular difference in degrees is computed as:</p>
<table id="A2.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RPE-R}_{i}=\arccos\left(\frac{\text{trace}(\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}^{T}\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i})-1}{2}\right)\cdot\frac{180}{\pi}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>RPE-R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>180</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RPE-R}_{i}=\arccos\left(\frac{\text{trace}(\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}^{T}\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i})-1}{2}\right)\cdot\frac{180}{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}=\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i+1}\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}=\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i+1}\mathbf{R}_{\text{ref},i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i}=\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i+1}\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mtext>est</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i}=\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i+1}\mathbf{R}_{\text{est},i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The RMSE over all consecutive frame pairs is:</p>
<table id="A2.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RPE-R}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\text{RPE-R}_{i}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RPE-R</mtext><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mtext>RPE-R</mtext><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RPE-R}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\text{RPE-R}_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preprocessing.</span>
Images are resized to <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="299\times 299" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>299</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>299</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">299\times 299</annotation></semantics></math> pixels for FID/FVD computation and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math> pixels for CLIP-based metrics, with appropriate normalization applied. For camera trajectory evaluation, images are resized to <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="720\times 480" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>720</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>480</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">720\times 480</annotation></semantics></math> pixels and uniformly sampled to 20 frames while preserving the first and last frames. Videos are uniformly sampled to 25 frames while preserving the first and last frames. For computational efficiency, FVD calculation further samples to 16 frames, and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CLIP</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLIP\text{-}V}_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math> processing is limited to 20 frames for long videos.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Protocol.</span>
For static scenes with multiple reference images, we directly construct the real distribution using all available images. For single-image scenes, we apply minimal augmentation strategies to avoid singular covariance matrices. Dynamic scenes maintain frame correspondence between generated and reference videos to ensure fair comparison. For camera trajectory evaluation, we employ Structure-from-Motion (SfM) to reconstruct camera poses from image sequences, then apply Sim3 alignment to handle scale ambiguity inherent in monocular reconstruction. Trajectory comparisons are performed using the <span id="A2.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">evo</span> toolkit with appropriate alignment and scale correction parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
